    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d505">

                <pb facs="0619" n="609" sameAs="frus:frus1947v07/pg609"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 Council/3–1047: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Smith</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Acting
                        Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Extracts]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1947-03-10T23:00:00+03:00">March 10, 1947—11
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received March 10—5:54 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>741. Delsec 1288. For the President and Acting Secretary Acheson from
                        Marshall.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d505-f1"/> The opening meeting of the Council
                    of Foreign Ministers convened this afternoon with Molotov,<anchor n="3"
                        xml:id="d505-f2"/> Bidault,<anchor n="4" xml:id="d505-f3"/> Bevin<anchor
                        n="5" xml:id="d505-f4"/> and myself present. [Here follows discussion of
                    procedural matters.]</p>
                <p>Molotov then proposed that the agenda include an information report by each of
                    the four signatory powers to the Moscow Declaration as to the fulfillment of
                    promises contained in the declaration as regards China.<anchor n="6"
                        xml:id="d505-f5"/> I stated that the submission of such a report appeared to
                    be a reasonable request but that I was concerned as to just how China would be
                    represented at the discussions. Molotov stated that since China was not a
                    signatory of the Moscow Conference he saw no reason why they should be
                    represented. (I do not think such report should be rendered to these four
                    Ministers, that is, to this Council, but only to Molotov and Bevin.)</p>
                <p>… Mr. Molotov then asked if I agreed to include the China problem in the agenda,
                    and I stated that I wished to think it over further and would report tomorrow.
                    …</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . .</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed> [Marshall] <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Smith</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d505-f1" n="2" xml:id="d505-f1-content">
